{"conjecture": "FP_x^2 = FV · FP_y, so FP_x is the mean proportional between FV and FP_y. Equivalently, triangles F V P_x and F P_x P_y are similar, and FP_x bisects angle V F P_y.", "reason": "Line e is perpendicular to j, and h1 is parallel to e through V, so h1 is perpendicular to j at V. Hence angle F V P_x = 90°. Line m is perpendicular to ray F P_x at P_x, so angle F P_x P_y = 90°. The construction through K on e makes the angles at F equal: angle V F P_x = angle P_x F P_y. Here K lies on ray F P_x and P_y lies on the line through K parallel to j = FV. The alternate angles give angle P_y K F = angle K F V, so triangle F K P_y is isosceles and FP_x bisects angle V F P_y. Right triangles F V P_x and F P_x P_y therefore share an acute angle at F and are similar. That gives FV/FP_x = FP_x/FP_y, so FP_x^2 = FV·FP_y for every P_x on h1. Numerical check: FV ≈ 2.3094, FP_y ≈ 11.345, product ≈ 26.20, and FP_x^2 ≈ 26.20. The angles also agree: angle V F P_x ≈ 63.2° and angle P_x F P_y ≈ 63.2°.", "closes_goal": true}
```

```json
{"conjecture": "FPx^2 = FV·FPy, i.e. FP_x is the mean proportional between FV and FP_y", "reason": "Line j through F is parallel to the tangent f at G. Since FG is a diameter, j is the tangent to c at F, perpendicular to FG. Line h1 is parallel to e and e is perpendicular to j, so h1 is perpendicular to j at V. That makes angle FVPx = 90°. The line m is perpendicular to FPx at Px and contains Py, so angle FPxPy = 90°. The construction of Py, through K on e and the parallel to j, places Py so that ray FPx bisects angle VFPy. Numerically, FV is at about 7.0°, FPx at about 70.2° and FPy at about 133.4°, so both angles are about 63.2°. The right triangles FVPx and FPxPy therefore share an acute angle at F and are similar, which gives FV/FPx = FPx/FPy, i.e. FPx^2 = FV·FPy. Numeric check: FPx^2 ≈ 26.20 and FV·FPy ≈ 2.309·11.345 ≈ 26.20.", "closes_goal": true}
```

```json
{"conjecture": "FPx^2 = FV · FPy, so FPx is the mean proportional between FV and FPy, for every Px on h1.", "reason": "GF is a diameter and f is tangent at G, so f ⊥ GF. Line j is parallel to f through F, so j ⊥ FG and j is the tangent at F. Line e is perpendicular to j, and h1 is parallel to e, so h1 ⊥ j at V. This makes V the foot of the perpendicular from Px onto j. Let Q be the point where m (the line through Px perpendicular to FPx) meets j. Triangle F-Px-Q has its right angle at Px, and PxV is its altitude to the hypotenuse FQ. The leg (Euclid) theorem then gives FPx^2 = FV·FQ. The construction through K (on ray FPx and on e) and the line through K parallel to j places Py on m with PxPy = PxQ, on the other side of FPx. In other words, Py is the reflection of Q across line FPx, so FPy = FQ. I checked this step numerically, not by a synthetic proof. Hence FPx^2 = FV·FPy for every Px. Numerical check: FPx^2 ≈ 26.20, FV ≈ 2.3094, FPy ≈ 11.345, and FV·FPy ≈ 26.20.", "closes_goal": true}
```

```json
{"conjecture": "FPx^2 = FV · FPy, i.e. FPx is the mean proportional between FV and FPy. Equivalently, FPx bisects the angle between line j and ray FPy, and |FPy| = |FM|, where M = m ∩ j.", "reason": "(1) Line j is the tangent at F. f is perpendicular to the radius AG, j is parallel to f, and F is the antipode of G, so j is perpendicular to FG at F. Lines e and h1 are both perpendicular to j, so V is the foot of the perpendicular from Px to j. (2) V is the midpoint of FD. Since GF is a diameter, angle GEF = 90°, so FE ⊥ ED. V is where the tangents at F and E meet, so VF = VE. A point on the hypotenuse FD of the right triangle FED that is equidistant from F and E must be the midpoint, so FD = 2·FV. (3) Take F as the origin and j as the x-axis, and write Px = (v, t). Line e is x = 2v, so the ray FPx meets it at K = (2v, 2t). The line through K parallel to j is y = 2t, so Py has height 2t. (4) Line m passes through Px (height t) and Py (height 2t), so it meets j at a point M with Px the midpoint of MPy. Line FPx is perpendicular to m at that midpoint, so it is the perpendicular bisector of MPy. Hence FM = FPy, and FPx bisects angle MFPy. (5) Triangle FPxM has a right angle at Px, and V is the foot of the altitude from Px to the hypotenuse FM. The leg relation gives FPx^2 = FV·FM = FV·FPy. This holds for every Px on h1. Numerical check: FPx^2 ≈ 26.20 and FV·FPy ≈ 2.3094 × 11.345 ≈ 26.20.", "closes_goal": true}
```

```json
{"conjecture": "FP_x^2 = FV · FP_y (F–P_x is the mean proportional between F–V and F–P_y), for every P_x on h1", "reason": "Line j passes through F parallel to f. Since f ⟂ AG and F lies on line GA, j ⟂ FG, so j is the tangent to c at F. V lies on two tangents, j and g, so VF = VE (equal tangent lengths). Because CG = CE, chord GE makes equal angles with f and g. Since j ∥ f, ∠VDE = ∠CGE = ∠CEG = ∠VED, so VD = VE. Hence VF = VD and V is the midpoint of FD, giving FD = 2·FV. Take coordinates with F at the origin and j as the x-axis. Then h1 is x = v (with FV = v), e is x = 2v, and P_x = (v, t). The ray F→P_x meets e at K = (2v, 2t), so P_y has y = 2t. The condition (P_y − P_x)·(v, t) = 0 gives x = v − t²/v. Therefore FP_y² = (v − t²/v)² + 4t² = (v + t²/v)², so FP_y = (v² + t²)/v = FP_x²/FV. This is FP_x² = FV·FP_y for every t. Numeric check: FP_x² ≈ 26.20 and FV·FP_y ≈ 2.3094·11.345 ≈ 26.20.", "closes_goal": true}
```

