{"conjecture": "FP_x^2 = FV · FP_y, i.e. FP_x is the mean proportional between FV and FP_y, for every P_x on h1.",
"reason": "Since FG is a diameter, f ⟂ FG, and j ∥ f, so j is the tangent to c at F. The lines e and h1 are both perpendicular to j, at D and at V. Because V lies on the tangent g, VF = VE. A short pole-polar argument (D lies on the chord GE, which is the polar of C) gives FD = 2·FV, so V is the midpoint of FD. Put F at the origin, with the u-axis along j and the n-axis along e. Then V = (a, 0), D = (2a, 0) and P_x = (a, t) with a = FV. The ray F P_x meets e (u = 2a) at K = (2a, 2t). So P_y, on the line through K parallel to j, has n-coordinate 2t. The condition (P_y − P_x) ⟂ FP_x gives P_y·P_x = |P_x|^2, so x·a + 2t^2 = a^2 + t^2 and x = (a^2 − t^2)/a. Then FP_y^2 = (a^2 − t^2)^2/a^2 + 4t^2 = (a^2 + t^2)^2/a^2, so FP_y = FP_x^2/FV. That is FP_x^2 = FV·FP_y, independent of t. Numerical check: FV ≈ 2.3094, FP_y ≈ 11.345, product ≈ 26.20, and FP_x^2 ≈ 26.20.",
"closes_goal": true}

